s still sitting here for me today.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Because You have a rescheduled debt hadn&#x27;t worked the actual task.</p><p>Speaker 8: Yet no.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Do they know what&#x27;s happening with it?</p><p>Paul Banyard: I just saw this morning that it was, but I had other priorities.</p><p>Bob Duncan: It&#x27;s been rebooked.</p><p>Bob Duncan: They&#x27;ve not updated that to the appointment.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Otherwise, they&#x27;ll never know. Obviously update yourself to the right date and time where it needs to be. And job done, but make sure you give them the feedback. Give them five days to go and check on that.</p><p>Speaker 8: Fine, I think we&#x27;re honest with some of those because we&#x27;ve got a bit of feedback. We&#x27;ve not given them feedback; how are they going to make any change?</p><p>Paul Banyard: I don&#x27;t even know if the client is showing us.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Here&#x27;s how it&#x27;s gone back into the diary. Customer meeting request.</p><p>Paul Banyard: I don&#x27;t know who did that.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Just double check my diary. I&#x27;m going to make a start today. I&#x27;ve caught up with some other bits and bobs. I&#x27;ve got an application to put on, but I&#x27;ll do that this evening.</p><p>Paul Banyard: So I&#x27;ve got an hour and a half before an appointment at two, and then I&#x27;ve got another appointment at half three, which should last till four. It might drag on, and then I&#x27;ve got another hour.</p><p>Paul Banyard: So I&#x27;ve got a good couple.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Of hours if I focus on that purely. The plan today was to clear platform tasks and get them up to date. Just for the practice as well. And then start chipping away at the Midas queue today. Didn&#x27;t anticipate getting through it today. But I&#x27;ve got more time tomorrow, currently Friday as well. So I&#x27;ll be back on track by Friday end of the week.</p><p>Paul Banyard: What&#x27;s your plan to make sure that you&#x27;re actually doing this single thing? I&#x27;ve added a little slot in the morning to give myself half an hour to review platform tasks. First thing when I log on at nine is emails, the most urgent ones. Half an hour for that, then half an hour for tasks. And primarily, just looking at what tasks I&#x27;ve got for the day, prioritizing them. Might not get through all of them in half an hour, but a good chunk of them. And you can always go and edit the task and say, &#x27;I am going to diary it for.&#x27;</p><p>Paul Banyard: Two o&#x27;clock, and it&#x27;ll pop back up as a task at two o&#x27;clock just so it&#x27;s not showing as overdue. I&#x27;ve got space blocked out in the morning just to prioritize those. And then obviously just through the course of working them, some of them will progress naturally as the workflow progresses. It&#x27;s documents requested or I&#x27;ve followed up with a client and I&#x27;m going to chase them in a week. It&#x27;ll be diarised for a week that sort of thing. So you&#x27;ll get that natural progression anyway.</p><p>Speaker 8: Yeah, yeah,</p><p>Paul Banyard: I&#x27;ve not. I&#x27;m not gonna pooh-pooh the benefits of it. Obviously it&#x27;s still getting used to it, but I&#x27;m getting quicker with it every day. You know how to navigate and find the information you want. It&#x27;s just keeping it as a priority when we&#x27;ve had this conversation numerous times. It&#x27;s plate spinning on that. You get pulled in a hundred different directions during the course of the day because you can say, right, I&#x27;ve got a bit of time here. I&#x27;m going to focus on my tasks that I&#x27;ve got sitting on platform. And then we get an email to do with Derek Orr, and that becomes a priority. Or you get an email from a client saying, here&#x27;s my documents. We&#x27;ve had an offer accepted or so that becomes up. Does that become more of a priority?</p><p>Paul Banyard: So.</p><p>Bob Duncan: And what about your</p><p>Bob Duncan: Just make sure all