ling a number out of thin air. I don&#x27;t know. Maybe a million pounds in mortgages. I literally have no idea.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Let&#x27;s convert that into prop fees. Let&#x27;s convert that into actually meaningful numbers that mean something to you here.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Potentially there could be a difference in getting closer to the target.</p><p>Bob Duncan: What number? There&#x27;s no right or wrong answer. I&#x27;m just asking what you think they might have lost out on by not following up.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Would you factor in some of them would take protection? Possibly, I don&#x27;t know three four thousand.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Did you say? I don&#x27;t.</p><p>Paul Banyard: But when I look through these, these are all people that I&#x27;ve been in contact with. I&#x27;ve chased. Some of these shouldn&#x27;t be here like Brianna Kennedy. We&#x27;ve already done the mortgage we&#x27;re tracing for protection review.</p><p>Bob Duncan: So let&#x27;s take that. I&#x27;m not going to go through every single one of them individually. What I&#x27;m trying to do is think about actually, what is the impact of not doing what we said we were going to do? You&#x27;ve got that number as you know, three, four or five. So maybe five is phone. We&#x27;ve got more than we have to before. Then how do you get back to people where you&#x27;re setting targets for June and thinking, well, the purpose of getting that back?</p><p>Bob Duncan: That&#x27;s what I want you to think about because at the end of the day, you&#x27;ve got a number to get to.</p><p>Paul Banyard: I&#x27;m constantly thinking of that. I can see where I sat last month.</p><p>Bob Duncan: But we&#x27;ve not done what we say.</p><p>Paul Banyard: It&#x27;s a point you&#x27;ve made. I think about it every day, Bob.</p><p>Bob Duncan: So that will be fixed by the end of the week. Set ten in your diary to do the tasks every single day. At the end of the day, you are going to make sure that all the tasks are worked. If you can&#x27;t get to, I guess the question is, if you know you are not going to get it, what are you going to do? You ain&#x27;t going to have a conversation with Sean or how are you going to make sure you are not putting that back tomorrow? Back tomorrow got this on my case now. And then we&#x27;re back at a week later and nothing&#x27;s changed.</p><p>Bob Duncan: So what is the action plan if we delve a little bit deeper? Because things like, perfect example, we need to tell the second about effects to not prepared. Let&#x27;s check those for candidates. So what is the plan? A shit has to find.</p><p>Bob Duncan: That kind of range was non-intentional.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Very good though.</p><p>Paul Banyard: My thought process is that it&#x27;s very easy and quick to look at your tasks. If you know the clients, you already have a good idea why it&#x27;s sitting there and what the task is. Whether it&#x27;s to chase them up about protection or whether it&#x27;s because they&#x27;ve found a property yet, whatever it might be. Or you&#x27;re trying to get them rebooked to get them a dep after an initial, but you can quickly review if you can&#x27;t remember.</p><p>Paul Banyard: See what it is, and then I make a judgment call. It&#x27;s like this important, but this one that Sean perhaps can do for me. So I can allocate it to Sean or ping him an email and say, look, can you do me a favor? Try to get this guy booked back in for a dep or whatever it is or chase for documents. Could you chase them up for me please? If it&#x27;s one that I need to deal with and I know I cannot get to it today, then just diarying on the task isn&#x27;t necessarily going to fix it because you don&#x27;t know what the next day holds. So for me, it would be blocking out time in my diary to specifically work that task as quickly as possible. So if I know I can&#x27;t, it&#x27;s mid afternoon. I&#x27;ve done all the other stuff, and I&#x27;ve got a couple of a few tasks left.</p><p>Paul Banyard: This isn&#x27;t one for Sean. This is one for me. But I can&#x27;t just keep diarying it on. Then I&#x27;ll put a remind. I would put a reminder in my diary, diary next day, half an hour. So I&#x27;ve got specific time allocated to work that specific client task. So it doesn&#x27;t keep getting diaried on.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Certainly, do you have time in your diary already for work?</p><p>Speaker 8: Yes. I can guess.</p><p>Paul Banyard: So you&#x27;ve already got the time allotted to deal with your work, boys? Therefore, you should be able to get them done in the day.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Or what&#x27;s the blocker? You&#x27;ve got ten minutes and you block it all. Say no, what&#x27;s stopping you? Distractions.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Not understanding what is actually a priority. So I might have time blocked out to do workflows, and then I get an email from you saying, or from someone else, we need to chase this up with Derek or whatever it is. Do I say, well, I&#x27;ll do that once I&#x27;ve done my workflow because I&#x27;ve got time allocated for that? Or is it really important that I respond to this client right now or deal with this particular case, even though it&#x27;s during time allotted for workflows? Maybe it&#x27;s a fault on my part that I don&#x27;t.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Fully understand which is a fucking stupid thing to say four years into this, you know what a priority is. I tend to work, you know, I&#x27;ll be honest. I tend to work what&#x27;s in front of my face. So I might I might start with good intentions and say,&quot; Right, I&#x27;ve got two hours in my diary today. I am going to get my Midas workflow cleared.&quot; But this is all stuff that I&#x27;ve chased numerous times before.</p><p>Paul Banyard: You know, and had no response, or they&#x27;re still looking, or whatever it is. Um, a lot of them you just get radio silence from. So, you think? Well, for me, it&#x27;s not really a priority because half the time when I work them, I never hear back from them anyway. Um so I&#x27;m going to focus on this. I&#x27;m going to get that Mortgage. Uh you know, I&#x27;ve got I&#x27;m going to put this Mortgage on and get this report done. And off my desk other, you know, or I get an email in from a client.</p><p>Paul Banyard: So, and that becomes a priority. So, you could you could you could phrase it as not understanding priorities. You could phrase it as being distracted,</p><p>Paul Banyard: Being Disorganized any numerous way you want to phrase it.</p><p>Paul Banyard: I Try to be organized. I try to, Got blocks in my diary for workflow. If I&#x27;ve got something urgent, I need to work on or it&#x27;s a reminder that I need to do this for this client, you know, Lender research, whatever it is. Um then I&#x27;ll maybe block out a better time, put a reminder in my diary for it as well.</p><p>Bob Duncan: And I think we just the hit the nail on the head there, it could be distracted. Maybe not quite tired, then kind of think about the previous to maybe sat down and you know, and get out of this thing. What is this? It&#x27;s probably about like how are worrying yourself and go like what is that am I to be doing just now?</p><p>Bob Duncan: Is it nice? I need to be chasing that for actually could I be going sure.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Can I take that before ten minutes, because usually this, because this has come on the desk, then. To get it done, good communication piece is kind of what I am picking up. That&#x27;s what I am picking the way. I don&#x27;t think that&#x27;s disorganized. They&#x27;re not prioritizing and they&#x27;re struggling to keep in the spring sides, But I also don&#x27;t think they&#x27;re taking full advantage of the support that&#x27;s sitting there on the background coaches getting because if you&#x27;ve got something that takes priority, Mortgage offer resale slipping it off in report versus following up with clients to see how they&#x27;re getting on.</p><p>Bob Duncan: The money is right there in front of you. That&#x27;s where your priority should be. The money, you know, keep one. That seems like that is why you do what to do. And it&#x27;s it&#x27;s the communication piece you need to be able to go Sean, I am going to grab you for ten minutes, and these five people I need them filled to see how they&#x27;re getting on, apologize that I can&#x27;t speak to them right now. But I&#x27;ve asked you to phone me for me. Sean will battle through that in ten minutes phone, the five people and they&#x27;ll get sent an email move, move the work forward. John, please.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Would you agree with that, Sean? Can I take more advantage of your services?</p><p>Sean McLennan: Absolutely. I probably find it better just knowing what&#x27;s going on anyway. If you&#x27;re saying, can I do this? And then update the workflow like that? I can probably bang that out in a few minutes, but it also keeps me updated on what&#x27;s going on with that client.</p><p>Bob Duncan: It&#x27;s like communication that is the key thing.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Quite interesting from what we&#x27;ve said. The whole point of this call was to get you to talk and tell me what you&#x27;re doing and what your event is. And it looks there were no right and no wrong answer in this. But the clear thing that I&#x27;m taking away is it&#x27;s that conversation piece, but I think that conversation needs to happen once a day, twice a day, three times a day. It&#x27;s going to be very dependent on what status we are in and what&#x27;s happening. And I think that&#x27;s probably the most important piece of that job.</p><p>Bob Duncan: I think you need to just be a bit more vocal and go. A nice way, I am spending weeks and I really want to keep in touch with these people. I really want to go back to those customers because it&#x27;s the mortgage they want to offer on the property. That kind of is the priority. But then what priority is this? You&#x27;re on this there folly as well. Got me again there. Actually, it&#x27;s a quick last week, two for my brothers. Can you do this for me, please? Perfect. And if you get any pushback or any tech back and somebody going no, can you tell me and I&#x27;ll be like Sean.</p><p>Sean McLennan: I&#x27;d rather you didn&#x27;t swear.</p><p>Bob Duncan: But I wouldn&#x27;t swear. As we&#x27;re shipping them out. Yeah, And I think, you know, whilst you said, three four thousand pounds in lost listeners, some of these people are a month old. I would argue that the, Probably a large amount of people potentially that have probably already gone and bought a house in the West. Yeah, yes, Because we know what I know that the average time scale from finding or having that conversation and finding a house is somewhere between fifteen to twenty five days.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Guaranteed, there will be some people on there that have bought that. We&#x27;ve just lost because we haven&#x27;t been in touch with them.</p><p>Bob Duncan: And that&#x27;s what frustrates me more, and that&#x27;s what shoots me. It frustrates me because it&#x27;s your numbers and it&#x27;s your business. At the moment, I&#x27;m kind of looking at your numbers and thinking, well, we&#x27;re behind in May. But come the tracking behind in June. What are we going to do? Because that is your banking income starting to slide. To ramp that back up in the other direction, we really need to ramp that up and pull that back. We are going to be coming into slightly quieter periods.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Summer holidays. And then it&#x27;s gonna get even quieter for October and November and December and January, and then it&#x27;s gonna ramp right back up. We kind of lost some extent the busiest periods or one of the busiest periods to the next busiest period that they might take over at once. So we need to think of a plan together to go, how do we get more opportunities on the other island?</p><p>Bob Duncan: How do we meet in the contacts and relationships?</p><p>Bob Dunca